.docname {Plugin Best Practices}
.docauthor {BakingRL}
.include {docs}
.doclang {en}

This guide turns the capability model into practical authoring rules. The goal
is to keep packages predictable in the host editor, safe at runtime, and easy
to maintain as the runtime API evolves.

## Choose The Right Capability

Use a visual for DOM rendering, animation, and frontend composition. A visual
can react to telemetry, settings, service results, and registry snapshots, but
it should not be the only owner of shared derived state.

Use a service for backend state. Services are the right place to aggregate
telemetry, publish package events, write registry snapshots, expose RPC methods,
and keep state that several visuals or pages need.

Use a connector for external systems. Request network hosts narrowly, keep
secret reads inside connectors, and move reusable package state into a service
when visuals need to consume the result.

Use a component when several visuals need the same browser UI fragment. Load it
through the host instead of importing another package directly.

Use page and layout templates to give users a starting point. Keep templates
portable by relying on package/export references and item settings rather than
specific template IDs.

Use a private configuration page only for package-specific workflows that need
more than generated settings fields. Leave secrets in the host-owned settings
UI.

## Design The Runtime Data Flow

Prefer one clear state owner. For telemetry-derived package state, subscribe in
a service, normalize the data, write a registry snapshot, and publish a
`plugin.<package-id>.*` event when visuals should update immediately.

Keep visual code instance-local. Store DOM nodes, latest render state, cleanup
callbacks, and settings per `context.root`, especially when a visual can appear
multiple times in the same layout or page.

Use service RPC for explicit requests, such as `snapshot`, `configure`, or
`reset`. Use bus events for streams and notifications. Use the registry for the
latest known shared value.

Keep RPC inputs and outputs JSON-compatible. They cross a host-mediated
boundary and should be easy to validate, serialize, and inspect.

## Make Editor Mode Useful

Editor mode is for visual authoring, not for simulating the full backend.

Provide a readable default state when the visual would otherwise be blank
without live data. For example, a scoreboard can show placeholder teams and a
score, while a goal animation can stay idle until the author triggers it.

Expose editor actions for meaningful states:

- different teams or sides;
- common scores or time states;
- transient animations such as goal and victory;
- empty, loading, and error states when they affect layout.

Do not publish editor actions to the real host bus. Use `context.editor.emit`
so the event reaches only the visual's own `context.bus.subscribe` handlers in
the editor renderer.

Avoid automatic transient triggers on editor mount. Starting with an idle or
default state makes the visual inspectable and lets the author trigger goal,
victory, or replay states intentionally.

Use preview mode when the question is whether the real chain works. Preview
should exercise the normal visual mount path, service calls, backend-derived
state, registry reads, and real host event permissions.

## Render Inside The Host Box

The host owns the outer rectangle. Render inside `context.root` and treat that
element as the sizing boundary.

For fluid widgets, use CSS that fills the root with `width: 100%` and
`height: 100%`.

For fixed-design broadcast graphics, keep an inner design surface at a known
size and scale it with a `ResizeObserver`. Disconnect observers and timers from
the cleanup returned by `mount`.

Implement `update(context)` when settings, opacity, metadata, or other
host-provided values affect the output. Remounting is a fallback, not the
normal refresh mechanism.

Do not position content against the viewport, mutate DOM outside
`context.root`, or depend on editor reloads to apply visual changes.

## Keep Permissions Narrow

Request only the events, registry keys, network hosts, and storage paths the
package needs.

Use package-scoped names:

- bus publish: `plugin.<package-id>.*`;
- registry keys: `plugin.<package-id>.*`;
- storage: `plugin://self/*`.

Treat telemetry, settings, registry values, RPC input, and component props as
untrusted. Validate or narrow values before using them in rendering,
state transitions, network calls, or persisted data.

Do not store secrets in package source, JSON settings, registry values, storage
files, generated bundles, logs, screenshots, or test fixtures. Connector
secrets should be read only when needed, and `configured(key)` should be used
for status checks that do not require secret material.

## Package For Maintenance

Keep the manifest, Vite inputs, source files, and generated `dist/` entries in
sync. Prefer `npm run add -- visual|component|service|connector <name>` for new
capabilities in generated packages.

Declare `compatibility.runtimeApi` explicitly and rebuild packages when the
runtime API changes. Do not rely on SDK version alone for compatibility.

Use `npm run validate` before packing or installing locally. Use
`bakingrl-plugin inspect` when you want to review the manifest surface and
effective permissions the host will see.

Document package-owned events, registry keys, settings, and permissions in the
package README. That keeps marketplace review and host debugging tractable.

